here  have been some really clear  guidelines uh developed in that sector  for avoiding um compact uh sorry erosion  and runoff in those cases  um looking into things like just  limiting uh weight and things like that  so in the landfill and Brownfield sector  you see people dealing with that  um  in terms of in the long term I think the  thing is that's important to understand  this is a really wide array of  um solar practices that can be used  during land uh development and part of  the question is also what practices are  people actually using and  what are the addition what would be the  additional cost of adopting you know  less impactful or more soil conserving  practices and part of that I think it  can hopefully be assisted by saying hey  we do want to maintain this as an  agricultural land either now or in the  future  um the other because one thing we have  talked a little bit about is whether or  not a solar uh so the lifetime solar  plant is about 30 years so one question  is you know can this be something that  is used you know temporarily and then we  say after 30 years we're going to pull  this out and do and and uh resume  cultivation on this land  um maybe after building some soil for  that for those decades um and I think  that also remains to be seen we're still  working on it  uh Roberta Nelson says relatedly to  Jan's question I'm curious what is known  about the impact of large-scale PV on  local Water Resources such as whether  water is needed to clean the panels how  water drains off the panels and any  impacts to Water Resources based on the  digging Drilling in into the pools are  these questions that have been answered  and researched or is it ongoing yeah so  we have research on on some of these so  uh for cleaning the panels I think the  answer is yes but not frequently so  there is panel cleaning but it's not  done very frequently it might not be  done at all actually in states with  higher rainfall so you know it's but it  is something that is often done in sort  of dustier more arid climates  um  you know one advantage that there's  there's no need to use drinking water  quality water for that and there's I  have seen some uh suggestions to use  sort of gray water or things like that  um I also know that I've also seen  um proposals to recycle or  um  otherwise gather collect that water and  perhaps use it for either watering in an  agriculture  um setting so you know clean the panels  and then irrigate with that water  um or potentially  um  just collect it for some other purpose  for water draining all the panels This  is a really interesting question uh from  a storm water management perspective we  do have a project called PV smart at  nrel  um which is uh I don't know what the  smart stands for but it stands for storm  water management something or other so  if you if you look that up uh I really  recommend it they've been doing a lot of  work uh especially looking at like local  zoning officials and talking to  environmental Regulators state level to  try to understand a what are the impacts  of solar on stormwater runoff do you do  potentially see like increased erosion  at the drip edge of the panel but maybe  there's you know there are ways  potentially to alleviate that or to work  against that or to try to counteract  that  um  finally for impacts to Water Resources  based on digging slash Drilling in the  poles  um I'm not really aware of that I mean I  don't think that they're usually deep  enough to to impact groundwater with  that but I'm I'm not sure I I'm not  aware of any research in that area  David bem asks any research or insights  on potential of battery storage sites  that would not be located near  transmission lines and where the  generated power would be temporarily  stored until it is dispersed to users  from the sewer generation site  yeah so that's like kind of an off-grid  or um maybe called micro grid solution I  mean I think it's certainly something  very interesting those Solutions tend to  be  well it's not to say like so if you look  at the microgrid stuff often in  isolation say wow that's that's so  expensive but because you have to build  all this extra storage and all this kind  of management  um and kind of electrical infrastructure  on the other hand if you're  way out there and you're gonna have to  build a transmission line miles and  miles it can look a lot it can look a  lot better so I think some of that is is  uh certainly difficult and as storage  costs decline will probably come into  perspective for communities I think the  other thing is if you're going to do  that it really one of the major  questions is what what size of load are  you hoping to serve I mean is that are  you open a service a town that's all  that's that's very far or has limited  transmission access are you just trying  to service that one farming facility and  depending on that is really going to  drive a lot of the questions of how you  would make that work  okay your last question Zach  uh thanks for this very clear and  helpful presentation can you speak a bit  to the embodied carbon of solar panels  materials used and lifespan  yeah  um it's great it's a very good question  I think it's always important to keep  this in mind I I think uh as we talk  about moving to like low or or zero  carbon sources we even remember this so  um I don't know offhand this these  embodied carbon numbers I do know that  from what I've been told most of the  carbon that's actually in solar well  there's a couple of sources one of it is  steel like you have all this racking and  stuff so some of it is about  decarbonizing steel production which we  need to do along the way and some and  some of it is about  um the electricity that's used in the  production process so what's good about  those is those are both  um kind of decarbonization problems that  people are are kind of focused on  separately  um and if we were if we had a  decarbonized Electric System and if we  had if we figure out how to build make  steel out of hydrogen or or electrical  reduction or whatever  um we would probably be able to produce  solar panels you know in a very low  carbon impact kind of way  um of course that's not the state we're  in now so that is something to mind I I  mean I certainly think that it Compares  there's there's no comparison with um  any of the fossil fuel sources  generation or anything like that it's  it's  um still a fairly low embodied carbon  um in terms of lifespan I think that  currently you know we I think use  usually 30-year lifespans as a standard  estimate I will say that a major  research Focus for us at this time is  trying to extend that  um to 40 or 50-year lifespans and the  purpose for that is really to say hey if  you're gonna you know the the a solar  panel or a solar farm has the  interesting kind of set up that it's all  investment right there's no fuel costs  the operational maintenance costs are  usually pretty low  um but there's this huge Capital cost up  front and so if you can make it last  longer than as an asset it sort of the  longer it lasts the more it pays off and  the more that um initial Capital cost  really pays pays for itself so there's  there's a there's an effort right now to  really improve the durability but um  I think 30 years is currently kind of  where we at now and 30 years might be  this is when it's economical to replace  the panels with newer more efficient  panels right it might not be you know  that doesn't mean that they're they're  they're  um necessarily garbage at that point  thank you so much Zach it was a  fantastic presentation  this has been a production of Cornell  University on the web at cornell.edu


